WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:13.400
TIMOTHY PEACOCK: Thanks for
joining us for Security 209,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.980
Cloud posture and
workload protection

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.080
with Security Command
Center Premium.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.230
I'm Timothy Peacock, a product
manager here at Google Cloud.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:22.670
I look after our
threat-detection capabilities.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:25.240
And in my spare time, I co-host
the "Cloud Security Podcast"

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.080
by Google.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.930
I'll be joined today by
Dr. Jasyn Voshell, director

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:30.700
of product and solution
security at Zebra.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.200
If you're interested
in learning how

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.283
to protect your organization's
use of Google Cloud

00:00:34.283 --> 00:00:36.730
as a platform and protect
your workloads on top,

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.440
this session is for you.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.420
We have a packed agenda
today-- an overview

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:43.600
of SCC Premium for those of you
new to the product, a deep dive

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.100
with our friend
Jasyn from Zebra,

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.690
and a lightning round
of announcements

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:49.630
from across the Security Command
Center platform, misconfig,

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.100
and threat-detection teams.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.640
Let's dive right in.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.640
Security Command Center
Premium brings together,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.890
in one central tool,
security insights

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:00.900
across your organization and
across the security lifecycle.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.870
I think of SCC Premium as
providing four pillars of value

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.280
for security teams.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.070
First, asset and
resource tracking.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.650
Staying current with resource
creation, update, and deletion

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.450
can be a challenge
in public clouds.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.580
If you choose to give
engineering teams flexibility

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.160
in creating new services,
Security Command Center's Cloud

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.280
Asset Inventory makes it really
easy for organizational admins

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.530
to stay aware of what's
changing inside their org

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:25.080
and easily query for
deleted and changed assets.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.730
Second, hardening.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.160
Misconfiguration and
vulnerability detection.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.080
Thanks to our real-time
asset updates,

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.170
SCC Premium provides real-time
misconfiguration detection.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.720
Cloud Security Posture
Management, CSPM,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.073
is a must-have for
security teams in Cloud.

00:01:39.073 --> 00:01:41.490
Security Command Center Premium
provides "out of the box,"

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.880
actionable posture insights
for our top GCP services.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.850
Understanding and responding
to misconfiguration exposure

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.718
is crucial for preventing
breach in Cloud.

00:01:50.718 --> 00:01:52.260
The third pillar is
threat detection.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.230
And we all know that despite our
best investments in hardening,

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.297
bad guys are still going
to try to do bad things.

00:01:57.297 --> 00:01:59.130
I'll talk a lot more
about detecting threats

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.420
in just a minute.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.030
Fourth and finally,
SCC Premium helps

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:05.430
you tie your success in Cloud
Security Posture Management

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.440
back to leading
compliance standards.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.240
Not only do our findings
map to leading standards,

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.618
we also provide the right
tooling in the platform

00:02:12.618 --> 00:02:14.160
to help you report
on your compliance

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.652
across your organization.

00:02:15.652 --> 00:02:17.610
Now let's look in-depth
at two of these areas--

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:21.410
first hardening, and
then threat detection.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.510
SCC Premium provides
"out of the box"

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.790
platform and workload hardening.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:29.180
Our posture findings are, like
I said, generated in real time

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.640
and come with
remediation instructions,

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.430
making these insights
usable and actionable

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.590
by engineering teams
and project owners who

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.990
are maybe not security experts.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.580
Above the infrastructure
layer, we're

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.480
now bringing in operating-system
vulnerability insights,

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:46.520
thanks to our partnership with
Google's VM Manager service.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.610
Users can expect to see more
updates on SCC's presentation

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:51.950
of CVE detection from
a couple of other teams

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.140
from Google Cloud really soon.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.260
Finally, on top of those
common vulnerability insights,

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.200
SCC Premium also
provides the same web-app

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.810
scanning for OWASP top
10 vulnerabilities,

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.510
as we use ourselves
in-house to protect Google.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.360
Our web-security scanner is
even easier to use than ever,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:11.570
with automated discovery and
scanning of your web apps.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:14.450
All of this comes together to
help security teams proactively

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.240
secure both their cloud
environment and their workloads

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.850
running on top without bringing
in third-party dependencies,

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.450
exposures, and risk.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.590
And with that, let's turn to
talking about the most unwanted

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:26.210
of third parties
with a quick look

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:28.280
at threat detection in Cloud.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:29.900
A few years ago at
conferences, we all

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.100
got to feel very smart
and smug by saying,

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.790
oh, the cloud is just
someone else's computer.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:37.370
The truth is, in Cloud,
there's a lot more than just

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:38.480
someone else's computer.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.070
We have new planes
of attack surface,

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:43.910
and with them, new opportunities
for instrumentation.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.620
At the same time as
the world of cloud

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.260
has brought tremendous
change to the security job,

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.880
our needs, as security
professionals,

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.540
haven't really
changed all that much.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.750
We're still focused on
keeping data confidential,

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.930
guaranteeing integrity,
and ensuring availability.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.420
Tolstoy wrote that,
quote, "All happy families

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:00.530
resemble one another.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:03.800
Every unhappy family is
unhappy in its own way."

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.920
Today's major public clouds
are our proverbial unhappy

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:07.580
families.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.010
Adversaries attack and pivot
through clouds differently.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.920
And security teams need deep
cloud-specific expertise

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.518
to detect the most dangerous
threats in their environments.

00:04:15.518 --> 00:04:17.060
With Security Command
Center Premium,

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.880
our customers get access to the
same real-time threat detection

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:23.750
as Google uses to protect
its own use of Google Cloud.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.360
They say all models are
wrong, but some are useful.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.310
Here, I offer what
is definitely wrong

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:30.980
but a hopefully useful,
six-layer model for thinking

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:33.830
about protect surface
in cloud environments.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.990
SCC Premium offers
detection modules

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:38.060
at each layer of
this six-layer cake,

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.240
with special emphasis on
detecting identity compromise

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:44.390
and protecting Kubernetes
workloads at runtime.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:45.860
We handle platform
threat detection

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:47.990
with our real-time
log-processing service, Event

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.360
Threat Detection, and
workload threat detection

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.380
with our Container
Threat Detection service.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.320
We'll talk more about
some new capabilities

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:57.240
in both of those
services in just a bit.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.030
With that, I want to welcome
our guest today, Dr. Jasyn

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:01.800
Voshell of Zebra Technologies.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.300
Jasyn, thanks so much
for joining us today.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.040
JASYN VOSHELL:
Thanks for having me.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:06.120
TIMOTHY PEACOCK:
Tell our audience

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:08.490
a little bit about yourself,
your role in the org,

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:09.717
and what your company does.

00:05:09.717 --> 00:05:10.800
JASYN VOSHELL: Absolutely.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.780
So I'm Dr. Jasyn Voshell.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.970
I'm the director for product
and solution security

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:16.620
for Zebra Technologies.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.900
Zebra may be the
biggest smallest company

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.160
you've never heard of.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.870
Everything from
scanning a package

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:23.550
to tracking an
asset from point A

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.980
to point B-- that's the
products we put out.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.750
And my responsibility
and accountability

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.910
is to make sure those
products we put out

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:31.155
are as secure as possible.

00:05:31.155 --> 00:05:32.880
A good example-- if
any of you have ever

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.730
gotten a package
delivered to you,

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.253
and it has a barcode on it,
that tracking from where

00:05:38.253 --> 00:05:40.170
it was all the way to
arrives at your doorstep

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:43.050
was using Zebra Technologies
products and possibly solutions

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:43.750
as well.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:45.960
And so my job is to, again,
make sure that everything

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.190
we do with that is as
secure as possible.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:51.280
TIMOTHY PEACOCK: That's
an amazing impact

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.710
that a company like
that has so invisibly.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:56.868
So they say to start secure
stories at the beginning.

00:05:56.868 --> 00:05:58.660
So could you tell the
audience a little bit

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:00.280
about getting started with
Security Command Center

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:01.830
Premium, how long
that took, and what

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:04.330
it was like rolling it out to
your different business units?

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.122
JASYN VOSHELL: Yeah,
it did take some time.

00:06:06.122 --> 00:06:08.205
I think with any new product
that comes into play,

00:06:08.205 --> 00:06:09.872
there's always some
resistance at first.

00:06:09.872 --> 00:06:11.950
People are used to doing
things a certain way.

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:14.230
But with the Google
Command Center,

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:16.810
we use a lot of GCP,
Google Cloud Platform,

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:19.060
in the solutions that we build.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.280
The old ways of trying to
make sure that's secure,

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:22.810
the apps on top
of it are secure,

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:24.930
tracking the inventory
of what we have--

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:26.540
it took a little bit of time.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:29.560
But as we progress
forward, it really helped.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:31.630
Good example-- we
had a customer,

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:34.360
during an RFP-- a Request
for Proposal-- discussion.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:35.830
And we were stepping through.

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:37.480
They were asking us, well,
how are you securing this?

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:38.500
How are you doing this?

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.120
And at one point,
we said, oh, we're

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.490
using the Google
Security Command Center

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:43.900
on top of this application.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:45.340
And they stopped
and went, well, I

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:47.632
don't think we need to ask
the next 10 or 20 questions,

00:06:47.632 --> 00:06:49.563
because they knew
about this product,

00:06:49.563 --> 00:06:50.980
and they knew that
those questions

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:52.210
were being answered already.

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:55.270
And so with that, when that took
a hold, when customers started

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:58.480
to realize, yeah, you're using
that, our internal teams went,

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:00.620
that's a lot faster.

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:02.050
We can answer questions quicker.

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:03.907
We can make sure
the risk is reduced

00:07:03.907 --> 00:07:05.990
or to an acceptable amount
where we need it to be.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:09.100
And with that, we were able to
get better internal adoption.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:11.470
You always want to carry
that big stick around,

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:12.762
but you never want to swing it.

00:07:12.762 --> 00:07:15.130
You want the teams,
internally, to want to use it,

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:16.790
to show them the value.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:19.240
And after a little bit of
that, getting over the hump,

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:21.550
having a customer go, yeah,
that makes it a lot easier,

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:23.830
having the sales
teams realize, hey,

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:26.200
you just answered an
RFP quicker on security

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:28.390
because you're
using this product--

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:29.950
they're happier about it, too.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:30.970
We got a lot of--

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:33.220
quicker adoption much easier.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:34.730
It also helped a great deal--

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:36.522
and I think we'll dive
into this a bit more

00:07:36.522 --> 00:07:40.510
in a second-- but being able to
give those teams back feedback.

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:43.805
Hey, we found something, and
here's how you can go fix it,

00:07:43.805 --> 00:07:45.430
and here's how quick
we gave it to you,

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:49.000
and not getting a lot of FUD
or a lot of cloudy stuff--

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.370
pun fully intended--
for those teams.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:53.570
So that does help,
across the board.

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:55.570
TIMOTHY PEACOCK: Yeah,
so let's dive in on that,

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:57.195
because one of the
things that comes up

00:07:57.195 --> 00:07:59.050
for me, with my
customers, is really,

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:01.180
how do they scale their
security operations as they

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.470
grow their cloud footprint?

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:04.480
One of the features
that we built--

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:06.220
really two things here--

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:08.260
to help address
that-- one is what

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:10.540
we call scoped or
delegated views of SCC,

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:13.000
where you can give the teams
responsible for a project

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.700
or folder a view of
their findings in SCC.

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:18.970
And the other is packing a
lot of detail and remediation

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:21.680
instructions into
the posture findings

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:24.710
so that non-expert teams
can really respond to them.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:26.920
So how has it gone rolling
out that delegation

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:28.090
of posture issues to teams?

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:30.340
JASYN VOSHELL: Yeah, so I
don't know if anyone has had

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:33.350
the old-school
way of, how do I--

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:36.220
IT or some team finds a
bunch of vulnerabilities,

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:38.450
and they want to give that
to the engineering teams

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:40.120
and say, go fix it.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:42.250
In the past, they might
have gotten a spreadsheet

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:43.480
or logged into a website.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.120
And it's just a
plethora of data.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:49.212
And anybody who looks at that
goes, like, my head hurts.

00:08:49.212 --> 00:08:50.170
I forgot what happened.

00:08:50.170 --> 00:08:52.095
And they kind of dismiss it.

00:08:52.095 --> 00:08:53.470
With the Security
Command Center,

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:58.030
the ability to focus in on
this business unit for this

00:08:58.030 --> 00:08:58.710
project--

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:00.400
so you're not looking
at every project,

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:02.627
just, hey, this business
unit, this project.

00:09:02.627 --> 00:09:04.960
Here are the things we're
finding for you to go look at.

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:06.550
It cuts right to the chase.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:09.220
And I think being able
to give those teams

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:12.340
or delegate to a certain
engineering team or security

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:14.320
team only what
they need to see--

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:15.760
They don't need
to see everything.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:17.930
In fact, if they do, they're
looking at the squirrel,

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:19.120
trying to figure out
where it's going.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:20.953
They can focus on
that, and they can

00:09:20.953 --> 00:09:22.120
fix it a lot quicker, right?

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:23.230
It's not too overburdened.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:26.290
So with that, it's helped
us speed up production,

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:28.332
in many cases, because
as we're moving--

00:09:28.332 --> 00:09:30.040
we'll talk about shift
left in a second--

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:33.010
as we're moving that further
into the development lifecycle,

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:34.275
we're finding things quicker.

00:09:34.275 --> 00:09:35.650
And we're only
showing the teams,

00:09:35.650 --> 00:09:37.220
this is what you need to fix.

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:39.080
And so it feels a
lot quicker for them.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:39.940
TIMOTHY PEACOCK:
Let's talk about shift

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:42.220
left, because we've got a
lot of different sessions

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.500
on shift left at
this year's Next.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:47.140
We've got talks about
supply-chain security

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:48.093
and securing that.

00:09:48.093 --> 00:09:49.510
Could you tell us
a little bit how

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:53.170
you see SCC's hardening work
supporting your organization's

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:53.992
shift-left efforts?

00:09:53.992 --> 00:09:55.450
JASYN VOSHELL:
Yeah, so shift left,

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:58.000
for anyone who's not familiar--
hopefully everyone is--

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.850
it means putting security
earlier in the development

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:01.460
lifecycle.

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:03.320
So as you're even
dreaming about a product

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:05.320
that you want to build,
you're thinking security

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:06.950
almost upfront, right away.

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:09.580
And so this tool allows
us-- so as we stand up,

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:12.070
even at development, a
non-production instance,

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:13.120
we stand up GCP.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:14.518
We put [INAUDIBLE] on top of it.

00:10:14.518 --> 00:10:16.060
That Security Command
Center is still

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:19.240
scanning that, still taking
a real-time type of look

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:20.350
into what we're doing.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:22.450
And we're able to fix
those items quicker.

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:24.250
And if anybody knows,
when you shift left--

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:27.190
because if I find a
vulnerability late in the game,

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.750
it's about to go out the
door, and suddenly I realize,

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:31.150
oh, no, the
foundation is cracked

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:33.730
on this house we already
built, it's a little late

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:34.990
to fix the foundation now.

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:37.970
Or if I'm going to fix it, it's
going to cost me quite a bit.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:40.720
And so we really want to do
those things early on, or left,

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:41.890
in the process.

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:44.890
The Command Center allows us
to find those vulnerabilities

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:47.780
quickly, all during the process.

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:51.060
So we're not, like, moving 10
feet and then doing a check.

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:52.650
Every time we're
making a little move,

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:54.240
it's always checking
on us so we can say,

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:55.240
hey, we found something.

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:56.880
Oh, we'll take care
of that right away.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:00.270
It helps to actually speed
up the development lifecycle

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.065
while keeping the
product secure.

00:11:02.065 --> 00:11:03.440
And that's very
important for us.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:04.530
It helps us get the
product out the door

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.780
quicker, which means
our customers have it

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:07.960
when they need it.

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:10.322
And that's the most
important for us.

00:11:10.322 --> 00:11:12.030
TIMOTHY PEACOCK: That's
such a nice story

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.705
around both helping
with security

00:11:13.705 --> 00:11:15.580
as well as helping with
development velocity.

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:18.300
So often, those are intention.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:20.190
Could we also talk a
little bit about how

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:22.273
this is being used at your
organization for threat

00:11:22.273 --> 00:11:24.450
detection and how that's
gone for your teams?

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.040
JASYN VOSHELL: Yeah, so one
of the nice things we like

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:29.730
to look at threat detection--

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:31.090
tons of tools in the world.

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:34.280
Everyone is going, oh, I can
find out what's going on here.

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:35.820
The hardest part, for me--

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:37.140
false positives.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:38.010
You see something.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:40.020
Hey, we think there's
a threat at the door.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:41.680
Well, is it or is it not?

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:44.400
And if you're making
me put a resource on it

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:47.490
to go investigate it,
you're chewing up resources.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:49.065
I can't expand my teams.

00:11:49.065 --> 00:11:52.210
The Security Command
Center, in my opinion,

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:54.865
has driven that false-positive
rate down quite a bit.

00:11:54.865 --> 00:11:56.490
[INAUDIBLE] with a
little bit of tuning

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:59.220
that we can do with the
help from Google to help

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:00.430
tune that as well.

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:04.590
So when we get an actual threat
or detection of a threat,

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:06.510
it feels more real.

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:08.820
We'll say, in the past,
you get a threat detection.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:12.330
Maybe there's a 40%,
50%, 60% confidence.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:15.060
When we see them now, through
the Security Command Center,

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:17.370
we're in the high 90s
on a confidence of,

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:18.870
yes, that is an actual threat.

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:19.740
It is actionable.

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:20.550
It's an event.

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:22.623
Let's go take care of that.

00:12:22.623 --> 00:12:23.790
What does that naturally do?

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:25.530
It frees up our
resources to do things

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:29.160
that are more important,
more risk-adverse, as opposed

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:31.680
to chasing ghosts, chasing FUD.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.920
So that helps us a
great deal, provides

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:36.150
confidence in the product.

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:38.822
It also helps us, even when
we're talking to our customers,

00:12:38.822 --> 00:12:40.530
telling them, yes,
we're using this tool.

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.570
OK, what kind of things
are you looking for?

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:43.560
We look for this.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:45.240
We're able to knock
things down quickly

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:48.300
and move to the next problem
or vulnerability or threat that

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.070
possibly comes up.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:52.470
Also, getting to the
false-positive piece,

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:53.970
it does allow--
again, it frees up

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:55.483
those resources,
which means they

00:12:55.483 --> 00:12:56.650
can go look at other things.

00:12:56.650 --> 00:13:00.237
So as teams are
developing a project,

00:13:00.237 --> 00:13:01.320
we're not chasing a ghost.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:02.570
We're looking at real threats.

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:04.000
And we can get
back to looking at,

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:05.340
is there a misconfiguration?

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:07.080
Are we now aligned
with a benchmark?

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:08.788
Is there a standard
we're not aligned to?

00:13:08.788 --> 00:13:10.530
And fix those things
to keep it hardened,

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:12.300
and not just chasing
things around.

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:13.800
TIMOTHY PEACOCK:
Oh, that's awesome.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:16.060
I want to touch on one
area before we wrap up,

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:17.820
which is the user interface.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:20.520
We've made a lot of changes
since some people started

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:21.270
using the product.

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:23.640
I understand you may have
seen the more recent versions.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.473
We've got a lot of good
changes coming soon.

00:13:25.473 --> 00:13:27.330
How is your team finding the UI?

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:29.110
JASYN VOSHELL:
Yeah, so you and I

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:30.360
have talked about this before.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:31.740
I don't like to call it a UI.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:35.700
I like to call it a UFI,
User Friendly Interface.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.790
The ability for anyone
to just log in on a whim

00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:42.300
and not have to crack open a
16-page or 160-page guide that

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:45.000
looks like some
major book and just

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:47.350
get to where you want to get
to is extremely important.

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:49.990
So as a director, I'm not in
the tool every day, right?

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:52.380
Our teams, our
SecOps division, is

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:54.150
in there looking at
what they need to do.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:55.440
They're more advanced on it.

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:56.910
But when we have
something come up,

00:13:56.910 --> 00:13:59.040
or leadership asks
a question on,

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:00.840
Jasyn, what's our
security posture?

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:02.340
What's our risk look like?

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:04.058
Or we have a customer
that says, we

00:14:04.058 --> 00:14:05.850
would like to know a
little bit more about,

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:07.725
what are you doing with
your vulnerabilities?

00:14:07.725 --> 00:14:09.090
How often do you see things?

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:11.380
As a director, I can
have the team open it up.

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:12.570
I can look inside.

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:15.030
And without really
knowing much more

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:17.220
than just my technical
space, I can figure out

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:19.350
what's going on in that
dashboard fairly quick.

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:22.050
And I've got to tell you,
that user-friendly interface

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:24.750
is very beneficial.

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:26.370
I, as a director,
can get an answer

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:27.930
really quick without
having to know

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:29.118
the technical details of it.

00:14:29.118 --> 00:14:30.660
Now, of course,
there are some things

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:32.820
that that team is better for.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.790
But from where I'm sitting,
I can get an answer quick.

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:38.250
I can tell our leadership,
our customers, or even

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.302
our internal people that
need those answers, even

00:14:40.302 --> 00:14:41.260
from where I'm sitting.

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:44.828
So I'm very much
a fan of your UFI.

00:14:44.828 --> 00:14:46.620
TIMOTHY PEACOCK: Jasyn,
I love that answer.

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:48.600
And with apologies to
directors of security

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:50.310
everywhere, I think
what we just heard

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.800
is that our user
interface is so easy, even

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:54.270
a director can use it.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:56.470
Jasyn, thank you so much
for joining us today.

00:14:56.470 --> 00:14:58.710
I really appreciate your
thoughts and insights

00:14:58.710 --> 00:14:59.850
on the product.

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:02.430
We didn't want to leave
out your CISO, either.

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:03.570
He's got this to add.

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:05.430
And what I love
about his quote here

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:08.880
is that it really shows how SCC
Premium brings together both

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:11.340
the Cloud Security Posture
Management side as well

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:14.190
as the Cloud Workload
Protection capabilities

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:17.430
to give your company really
a one-stop shop for all

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:19.620
your needs in
protecting Google Cloud.

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:23.310
So I promised announcements,
and I didn't forget.

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:25.140
We've got a couple
of new capabilities

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:26.940
that are exciting
in the platform

00:15:26.940 --> 00:15:29.490
as well as in the hardening
and threat-detection sides

00:15:29.490 --> 00:15:30.220
of the house.

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:34.240
So with that, a couple of
really great announcements here.

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:36.990
The first one is that I've
had so many users ask me,

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:38.880
over the years, how
they can tune and manage

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:42.000
our systems that I suspect
security engineering is

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:43.400
actually full of musicians.

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:45.150
We're rolling out a
feature now that we're

00:15:45.150 --> 00:15:46.980
calling Mute, which
is essentially

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:49.500
Gmail filters for
your SCC environment.

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:51.480
We know that not every
one of our findings

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:53.400
is always right for
your environment.

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:55.560
Organizations can have
compensating controls,

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:58.200
and threat intelligence
is occasionally noisy.

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:01.530
With the Mute feature, teams
can easily silence and control

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:03.450
the scope of those
silences to ensure

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:05.880
that their findings
queue is full of findings

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:07.530
that teams care about.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:09.870
Muting would be really hard
to get right without finding

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:10.900
standardization.

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:12.690
And so we've
standardized attributes

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:14.130
across our different findings.

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:17.310
With Mute, we're simultaneously
rolling out new attributes

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:19.710
and making sure that our
vulnerability and threat

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:21.870
teams are using
those to give you

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:25.412
a consistent experience as you
go through the findings set.

00:16:25.412 --> 00:16:27.120
We know that teams
already have workflows

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:28.495
for their other
security tooling.

00:16:28.495 --> 00:16:30.360
And we're making
it easier than ever

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:33.490
to export SCC's findings
into your channels of choice,

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:36.370
including, now, your
Slack channels of choice.

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:39.360
Finally, a big ask from users
has been scoped or delegated

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:39.900
views.

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:41.640
Jasyn and I touched
on their value.

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:44.700
And using the normal IAM
patterns from Google Cloud,

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:47.280
teams can now give teams
responsible for projects

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:48.930
and folders insights
into the issues

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:51.730
happening in those environments.

00:16:51.730 --> 00:16:53.397
On the misconfig and
vulnerability side,

00:16:53.397 --> 00:16:55.605
we've got some really exciting
announcements as well.

00:16:55.605 --> 00:16:58.120
Security Health Analytics, our
real-time posture-management

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:00.813
system, now supports
user-defined detection.

00:17:00.813 --> 00:17:02.230
This has been a
top ask, and we're

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:04.550
really excited about
what's now possible.

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:07.359
I mentioned that we're bringing
in OS-level CVE detection

00:17:07.359 --> 00:17:08.713
with our VM Manager integration.

00:17:08.713 --> 00:17:10.630
This is a great collaboration
with our friends

00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:13.060
over on Google Compute Engine
and will help organizations

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:14.800
to understand how
well they are doing

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:17.500
at the eternal
topic of patching.

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:20.533
It's a bit like flossing-- not
exciting, not necessarily fun,

00:17:20.533 --> 00:17:22.450
but definitely among the
most important things

00:17:22.450 --> 00:17:24.829
you can do to shift
your security outcomes.

00:17:24.829 --> 00:17:27.530
Finally, on the misconfig
and hardening front,

00:17:27.530 --> 00:17:29.830
we're also bringing
in detection of CVEs

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:33.010
in widely-deployed applications
with a managed deployment

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:35.520
of our open-source Tsunami tool.

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:38.350
Now, with that, on to the
threat-detection updates--

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:41.240
three areas in Event
Threat Detection and one

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:43.210
in Container Threat Detection.

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:45.670
Google Groups is used
by most organizations.

00:17:45.670 --> 00:17:48.010
Rather than assign IAM
roles directly to users,

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:49.870
roles are assigned
to groups, and users

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:51.100
belong to those groups.

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:53.225
This makes management of
onboarding and offboarding

00:17:53.225 --> 00:17:55.360
a lot easier, but there
are some gaps and risks

00:17:55.360 --> 00:17:57.340
that get introduced
by Google Groups.

00:17:57.340 --> 00:17:59.590
ETD has now integrated
with Google Groups

00:17:59.590 --> 00:18:01.360
to give teams findings
that highlight

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:04.240
when a group with permissions
becomes more widely joinable,

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:07.270
as well as when a grant
is made to a group that

00:18:07.270 --> 00:18:09.110
contains external members.

00:18:09.110 --> 00:18:10.840
Additionally, we've
recently released

00:18:10.840 --> 00:18:14.380
a set of detection techniques
using User Entity Behavioral

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:16.390
Analytics, or UEBA.

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:18.880
It's one of those acronyms that
only our friends at Gartner

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:19.450
could love.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:21.280
But it describes the
general technique

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:24.700
of profiling and detecting
deviation from those profiles.

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:27.040
We're using it today to
detect account takeover

00:18:27.040 --> 00:18:28.930
and compromise for
both IAM accounts

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:31.060
as well as service accounts.

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:33.700
On the Kubernetes side,
we're rolling out a number

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:34.990
of new detection modules.

00:18:34.990 --> 00:18:37.060
One that we released
very recently

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:40.240
is a machine-learning-powered
bash-script classifier.

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:42.280
Our general theory
of defense with KTD

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:45.940
is to take away from adversaries
entire classes of technique

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:47.770
rather than specific procedures.

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:51.370
With this detector, we have
a robust and generic approach

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:54.790
to detecting malicious script
execution inside of containers.

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:56.980
It leverages Google's
strengths in ML,

00:18:56.980 --> 00:18:58.660
and we're really
excited about how

00:18:58.660 --> 00:19:02.970
it's performing against
production data already.

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:04.093
One more thing.

00:19:04.093 --> 00:19:05.760
We've integrated
Security Command Center

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:07.440
Premium with Chronicle.

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:09.210
This is a wonderful
case of the teams

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:10.980
truly being better together.

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:13.080
SCC's real-time
threat detection joins

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:15.930
Chronicle's powerful
investigatory capabilities.

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:18.330
With this integration,
users can seamlessly

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:21.060
pivot from a [INAUDIBLE] detail
in Security Command Center

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:24.510
into a curated alert
view within Chronicle.

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:26.700
From there, users can
investigate the threat

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:29.400
and quickly pivot to
associated actions and events

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:31.200
related to the finding.

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:34.920
For example, imagine a SOC
operator quickly investigating

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:38.290
if a principal observed making
a suspicious IAM grant had

00:19:38.290 --> 00:19:41.640
recent login activity or if they
made other suspicious changes

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:43.872
after that IAM grant.

00:19:43.872 --> 00:19:46.330
We're really excited about
where this partnership is going.

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:48.400
Beyond ingesting SCC
findings, Chronicle

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:51.400
is also ingesting the stream of
Cloud audit logs in real time

00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:54.550
and pulling Cloud Asset
Inventory metadata to ensure

00:19:54.550 --> 00:19:56.710
that analysts have the
latest and greatest context

00:19:56.710 --> 00:19:59.488
when investigating threats.

00:19:59.488 --> 00:20:01.030
I know this has been
a whirlwind tour

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:03.640
of SCC Premium, our
capabilities, and what's new.

00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:05.080
Thanks for hanging on.

